{
 "_source": "https://github.com/vectara/hallucination-leaderboard (README of the repo; the table is rendered there)",
 "_retrieved": "2026-09-29",
 "_table_last_updated": "September 22, 2026 (stated in the README)",
 "_basis": "MEASURED by Vectara, single-vendor, single-task. Each model is asked to summarize short source documents; the summary is scored by HHEM-2.3, Vectara's COMMERCIAL hallucination-detection model. LOWER hallucination_rate_pct IS BETTER (0 would be perfect). factual_consistency_rate_pct is its complement (100 - hallucination rate) and carries no extra information -- it is retained only as a parse check, and all rows satisfy it. answer_rate_pct is the share of documents the model produced a summary for AT ALL; a low hallucination rate at a low answer rate is abstention, not accuracy, so the two MUST be read together. HIGHER answer_rate_pct is better. Caveats that bound how far this transfers: (1) it is grounded summarization of SHORT documents, not open-domain factuality, so it does not predict fabrication in generation or extraction tasks; (2) the judge is itself a model, so the rate is HHEM-2.3's detection rate, not ground truth; (3) HHEM-2.3 is closed -- the open variant on HuggingFace is HHEM-2.1-Open and is NOT the model that produced these numbers, so the scoring cannot be independently reproduced.",
 "_selection": "THE WHOLE TABLE, every row, unfiltered. Vectara publishes no parameter count and no license field, so no size or openness cut was applied.",
 "_coverage_note": "Rows were parsed from Fetcher's readable-text extraction in fixed 5-token groups (name, hallucination %, factual consistency %, answer %, avg length) after the verified 5-column header. Validated by requiring hallucination + factual consistency = 100 on every row: 0 violations. No field in this file is NOT_OBTAINED.",
 "_not_obtained": [
  "parameter count (not published by Vectara)",
  "quantisation / serving precision (not published by Vectara)",
  "per-model confidence interval or run count (not published by Vectara)",
  "the source-document set and the HHEM-2.3 weights (both closed)"
 ],
 "_dataset": "Stated in the README: over 7,700 articles spanning news, technology, science, medicine, legal, sports, business and education; lengths from ~50 words to ~24K words; DELIBERATELY NOT PUBLICLY AVAILABLE, to avoid overfitting. So the inputs cannot be inspected and the scoring cannot be rerun -- this is a trust-the-vendor measurement.",
 "_ordering_note": "Rows are in the README's own published order (roughly ascending hallucination rate) but that order is NOT strictly sorted: it inverts at three places, verified against the raw text -- gpt-6-sol 6.5% after command-r-plus-08-2024 6.9%; ministral-8b-2410 7.4% after gpt-6-astra 8.7%; gpt-5.1-high-2025-11-13 12.1% after gpt-5.6-sol 12.4%. These are Vectara's, not a parse artifact. Sort on the field, never on position.",
 "_abstention_examples": "Why answer_rate_pct must be read with the hallucination rate, from this table: snowflake-arctic-instruct scores 4.3% hallucination but answers only 62.7% of documents, and microsoft/Phi-4 scores 3.7% at an 80.7% answer rate -- both rank better on hallucination than models that answered essentially everything.",
